Falsifies `system_tests::user::recv_cap_attack_tests::an_interrupt_signal_received_by_recv_cap_delivers_no_cap_whichever_side_parks_first`.

Architecture: all three (the change is in shared scheduler code, `kernel/src/sched.rs`).

The defect this restores is the receiver-first order of an interrupt signal: `irq_notify` drops
`[1, 0, 0, 0, 0]` into a parked receiver's mailbox, and with the `cap_delivered` default reverted
`ipc_recv_cap` returns it unchanged, so `x1 = 0`, where the signal-pending order returns `NO_CAP`.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(RECEIVER_X1, abi::rendezvous::NO_CAP, "an interrupt signal handed the
receiver x1 = 0 on the receiver-first order ...")`. The `w0 == 1` check before it and the
signal-first half after it stay green, which is what makes the red about the order.

The patch is the hunk of milestone 634 (a plain SEND received by RECV_CAP never hands the receiver a
sender-chosen slot), byte for byte: `ipc_recv_cap`'s receiver-first read is the one place all three deliveries
(a plain SEND, an interrupt signal, a death message) meet, so reverting it is the only defect that
puts back what this test pins. That the same revert turns the interrupt test red is the evidence that this
test, and not only 634's, reaches the default. The hunk's context cites milestone 151 (notification
objects: async multiplexing without wait-any), whose bound-notification tag the fix exempts, and milestone 706 (a `CALL` server can tell a Reply from a delegation),
whose `x4` comment sits in the hunk's context.

Replayed on 2026-10-03 (UTC) by the lane for milestone 714 (the sibling RECV_CAP paths get a receiver-first test); see the roadmap block for the exit code and
the assertion line.

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched.rs b/kernel/src/sched.rs
--- a/kernel/src/sched.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched.rs
@@ -3827,17 +3827,7 @@
             // receive, `abi::rendezvous::REPLY_DELIVERED` when a CALL's Reply was installed
             // (milestone 706, written by `ipc_call_badged`), and `0` for every other delivery a
             // sender or the kernel's death path makes (milestone 151).
-            // **x1 is NO_CAP unless a capability was installed for this delivery** (milestone 634,
-            // fatal risk 7). A plain SEND that reached us parked drops its three words straight into
-            // the mailbox, so m[1] is the sender's chosen word; returning it would hand a sender a
-            // slot number where a CALL server reads a reply slot. A bound-notification delivery is
-            // exempt: its x4 == BOUND and its m[1] is the notification word, not a sender's choice.
-            let m = t.mailbox;
-            if t.cap_delivered || m[4] == abi::notification::BOUND {
-                m
-            } else {
-                [m[0], NO_CAP, m[1], m[3], 0]
-            }
+            t.mailbox
         }
     }
 }
